---
import Desktop from "../layouts/Desktop.astro";
import { repo, version, releasedText, changelog, download, downloadZip, requires, checkedWith, driftNote, stage, sha256 } from "../data/site";
import { orgNode, webPage, breadcrumbs } from "../data/schema";

const title = "Install cl_audit, a free Claude usage app for Mac";
const description =
  "Download cl_audit for Apple silicon Macs (macOS 13 or later), allow it once, and add a profile for each Claude account. It's free and needs Claude Code.";
const jsonLd = [
  webPage({ path: "/install/", name: title, description }),
  breadcrumbs([{ name: "Home", path: "/" }, { name: "Install", path: "/install/" }]),
  orgNode,
];
---
<Desktop title={title} description={description} page="install" headline="Download or build from source" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <div class="content-inner">
    <!-- two columns that each flow on their own (no rows, so a short card leaves no gap): installing it and
         adding a profile on the left; building it yourself, what it needs and keeping it up to date on the right.
         One column on narrow windows, in reading order (the i-* order classes). -->
    <div class="install-cols">
      <div class="col-stack">
        <div class="card dl-card i-1">
          <h4>Download</h4>
          <!-- as on the Overview's Install card: the key, a divider, what it is -->
          <div class="key-note dl-row">
            <a class="pill dl" href={download}><span class="dl-l">Download for Mac</span><span class="dl-na">Mac only</span></a>
            <span class="divider" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span class="note">cl_audit {version}{stage && ` ${stage}`} · {releasedText()} · {requires} · <a href={changelog}>What's new</a> · <a href={downloadZip} style="white-space:nowrap">or the .zip</a></span>
          </div>
          <p class="t login-note" style="margin:0 0 12px"><b>Claude login:</b> sign-in happens in Claude's own screens, so cl_audit never sees the password. It reads Claude Code's saved login read-only and sends it only to Anthropic. <a href={`${repo}#security-how-cl_audit-handles-the-claude-login`}>How it works</a></p>
          <div class="steps">
            <div><div><b>Open the disk image and drag cl_audit onto Applications</b><span>Double-click <code>cl_audit.dmg</code> in Downloads; in the window that opens, drag cl_audit onto the Applications folder. With the zip, double-click it and drag cl_audit onto Applications.</span></div></div>
            <div><div><b>Open it; macOS asks once</b><span>cl_audit isn't signed with an Apple Developer ID yet, so the first time macOS says it can't verify the app. Click Done.</span></div></div>
            <div><div><b>Allow it in Privacy &amp; Security</b><span>Open System Settings ▸ Privacy &amp; Security, scroll down and click <b>Open Anyway</b> next to cl_audit, then confirm. You only do this once; the Dock icon apps cl_audit makes for your profiles open without asking.</span></div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="gk" aria-hidden="true">
            <div class="gk-title">Privacy &amp; Security</div>
            <div class="gk-row"><span>“cl_audit” was blocked to protect your Mac.</span><span class="gk-btn">Open Anyway</span></div>
          </div>
          <p class="t sha">Every release comes as a disk image and a zip, with the same app and the same code signature. To check the download, run <code>shasum -a 256 ~/Downloads/cl_audit.dmg</code> (or <code>cl_audit.zip</code>). For cl_audit {version} it prints:</p>
          <div class="sha-list">
            <div><span>cl_audit.dmg</span><code class="sha-v">{sha256.dmg}</code></div>
            <div><span>cl_audit.zip</span><code class="sha-v">{sha256.zip}</code></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="card i-1">
          <h4>First run: Set up cl_audit</h4>
          <p class="t">With no profile yet, cl_audit opens <b>Set up cl_audit</b>, three steps that each say what they need:</p>
          <div class="steps">
            <div><div><b>1 · Claude Code</b><span>Found, with its version and where it is. If it isn't on this Mac, the page shows the official install command with a <b>Copy</b> key, a link to the install page and <b>Check again</b>; it also looks again by itself, so installing Claude Code needs no restart. With only the Claude app, cl_audit can fall back on the copy of Claude Code the app keeps for itself; a real install is preferred.</span></div></div>
            <div><div><b>2 · The account</b><span>The first profile is the default one: <code>~/.claude</code> with the usual Claude app: what Claude shows when you open it from the Dock, and what Claude Code in Terminal uses. If <code>~/.claude</code> is already signed in, it's offered first with its address, and <b>Use</b> takes it as it is. If not, <b>Sign in…</b> opens Claude Code's own browser sign-in in Terminal and signs in <code>~/.claude</code>; the page goes on by itself once the account shows up. Other accounts come later, as more profiles (a folder and Claude window of their own) or as standby accounts.</span></div></div>
            <div><div><b>3 · Name and colour</b><span>Filled in from the account: a team's name, or the first part of the address. <b>Done</b> adds the profile and opens its settings.</span></div></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="card i-4">
          <h4>Add a profile for another Claude account</h4>
          <div class="steps">
            <div><div><b>Settings ▸ ＋ (New profile)</b><span>After the first profile, you add more here. A page opens with the profile's Claude window and Dock icon switches, the Claude Code card, and the numbered steps.</span></div></div>
            <div><div><b>Pick its account</b><span>Claude Code ▸ <b>Account</b> lists the accounts already signed in to a Claude Code folder on this Mac that no profile uses. Picking one connects that folder as it is: its sign-in, settings and history stay. For any other account, pick <b>Sign in to another account…</b>, type its address and press <b>Sign in</b>: cl_audit makes a new folder, <code>~/.claude-&lt;name&gt;</code>, which can share instructions, agents, skills, commands or memory from another folder.</span></div></div>
            <div><div><b>Choose what it includes</b><span>Two switches: <b>Claude window</b> (with <code>~/.claude</code>, the usual Claude app; with any other folder, its own Claude window, with its own sign-in, chats and settings) and <b>Usage in the Dock</b> (a Dock icon that shows its usage). With both off the profile is usage only: its usage shows on the dashboard, in the launcher and in alerts.</span></div></div>
            <div><div><b>Quit the Claude app until the folder is set up</b><span>Only for a profile with a Claude window: while the Claude app is open, it writes its own sign-in and settings. The page says what's open and checks every 2 seconds. Claude Code in Terminal doesn't get in the way. With no Claude window, the step isn't there.</span></div></div>
            <div><div><b>Sign in, in two parts</b><span>Claude Code first: Terminal opens Claude Code's own sign-in for the folder, with the address filled in if you typed one. The browser's claude.ai account decides which account it is, so switch it there first if it shows another one. Then, if the profile has a Claude window, you sign that window in to the same account; its Code sidebar starts empty. “Finish now” leaves sign-in for later. One profile per account, checked by account and organisation: an account another profile has is refused. A personal plan and a Team seat on one email are two accounts.</span></div></div>
            <div><div><b>Add the Dock icon, or start reading usage</b><span>With Usage in the Dock, cl_audit installs “cl_audit &lt;profile&gt;” and puts it in the Dock. Settings ▸ General ▸ Dock can keep the icons in order for you (experimental: it rewrites the Dock's pin list and restarts the Dock). Without it, <b>Start reading usage</b> installs nothing, because cl_audit's background agent reads every profile. The first time, macOS says cl_audit added a background item and asks once to allow notifications for cl_audit.</span></div></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="col-stack">
        <div class="card i-2">
          <h4>Or build it from source</h4>
          <p class="t">Review the source, then build on the Mac:</p>
          <pre class="code"><code>git clone {repo}.git
cd cl_audit
git checkout v{version}   # optional: exactly this release
./build.sh</code></pre>
          <p class="t">
            build.sh compiles cl_audit, installs it in /Applications and updates the Dock icon apps: one
            “cl_audit &lt;profile&gt;” app per profile, next to it, where Spotlight finds them. <code>./build.sh --no-install</code> only
            builds into the folder. Built on your own Mac, it opens without the Privacy &amp; Security step.
          </p>
          <div class="key-note"><a class="key" href={repo}>GitHub</a><span class="divider" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="note">needs the Xcode Command Line Tools</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="card i-3">
          <h4>Requires</h4>
          <div class="srow"><span>Checked with</span><span class="v">Claude Code {checkedWith.claudeCode} · Claude app {checkedWith.claudeApp}</span></div>
          <div class="srow"><span>Mac</span><span class="v">Apple silicon</span></div>
          <div class="srow"><span>macOS</span><span class="v">13 Ventura or later (the widget: 14 Sonoma)</span></div>
          <div class="srow"><span>Claude Code</span><span class="v">installed (if only the Claude app is there, cl_audit can use its copy)</span></div>
          <div class="srow"><span>Per profile</span><span class="v">Claude Code, signed in</span></div>
          <div class="srow"><span>Optional</span><span class="v">the Claude app, for Claude windows</span></div>
          <div class="srow"><span>Plans</span><span class="v">Pro, Max, Ultra or Team (plans with usage limits)</span></div>
          <div class="srow"><span>To build it yourself</span><span class="v">Xcode Command Line Tools · <code>xcode-select --install</code></span></div>
          <p class="t drift">{driftNote}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card i-5">
          <h4>Update, move, remove, uninstall</h4>
          <div class="steps">
            <div><div><b>Update</b><span>Download the new disk image (or zip) and replace cl_audit in Applications (<a href={changelog}>what's new</a>), or run <code>git pull && ./build.sh</code> in the cl_audit folder. The Dock icon apps follow, and profiles and settings are kept.</span></div></div>
            <div><div><b>Move to another Mac</b><span>Settings ▸ any profile ▸ Sessions ▸ Export… ▸ <b>Move to another Mac</b> puts most of your setup in one password-protected disk image: every profile's sessions and Claude Code setup, and cl_audit's own profiles and settings. Your project folders don't travel. Put them on the new Mac at the same paths first (clone or copy them), or the moved sessions can't be picked up again. Rewind checkpoints don't travel either. On the new Mac, install cl_audit, go through its first run, then Import… that file. Both Macs need 26.11.10 or later: older versions can't open a move file. Sign-ins don't travel, so you sign in to each account again. The <a href="/settings/#move-to-another-mac">settings demo</a> shows the page.</span></div></div>
            <div><div><b>Remove a profile</b><span>Settings ▸ the profile ▸ Remove profile… removes its Dock icon app and what macOS keeps for it, plus its saved usage and the alert marks cl_audit keeps for it. “Delete its folders too” also signs its Claude Code folder out and deletes it and its Claude window's own folder; the default <code>~/.claude</code> and the usual Claude app's data are never deleted.</span></div></div>
            <div><div><b>Uninstall</b><span>Settings ▸ General ▸ <b>Uninstall cl_audit…</b> (or the cl_audit menu) removes every Dock icon app and its pin, the background agent, saved usage, settings and caches, then moves cl_audit to the Trash. For each profile you choose whether its folders go too. From Terminal, <code>/Applications/cl_audit.app/Contents/MacOS/UsageDock --uninstall</code> lists what would go, and adding <code>--yes</code> does it. Any leftovers in between are tidied up by the background agent, which removes only what is provably cl_audit's.</span></div></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Desktop>

<style>
  .install-cols { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 14px; align-items: start; min-width: 0; }
  .install-cols .card { min-width: 0; }   /* long code lines scroll inside their box instead of widening the page */
  .col-stack { display: grid; gap: 14px; align-content: start; min-width: 0; }
  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .install-cols { display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: stretch; gap: 14px; }
    .col-stack { display: contents; }
    .i-1 { order: 1; } .i-2 { order: 2; } .i-3 { order: 3; } .i-4 { order: 4; } .i-5 { order: 5; }
  }
  .card .t + .key-note { margin-top: 12px; }   /* the GitHub key a little apart from the text above it */
  .drift { margin: 10px 0 0; font-size: 12.5px; }
  .dl-row { margin: 2px 0 14px; }
  .sha { margin: 12px 0 0; font-size: 12.5px; }
  .sha-list { display: grid; gap: 4px; margin: 6px 0 0; font-size: 12.5px; }
  .sha-list > div { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 96px minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 8px; align-items: baseline; }
  .sha-list span { color: var(--muted); }
  .sha-v { word-break: break-all; }   /* the 64-character digests wrap on a phone instead of widening the page */
  @media (max-width: 520px) { .sha-list > div { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 0; } }
  /* the macOS Privacy & Security row, as macOS draws it */
  .gk { margin: 2px 0 0 46px; border-radius: 9px; background: #2b2b2e; box-shadow: 0 0 0 1px rgba(255,255,255,.08), 0 6px 16px rgba(0,0,0,.3); padding: 10px 12px; font-size: 13px; }
  .gk-title { font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; }
  .gk-row { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: 10px; color: var(--muted); flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .gk-btn { padding: 3px 12px; border-radius: 6px; background: rgba(255,255,255,.14); color: #fff; font-weight: 600; box-shadow: 0 0 0 2px rgb(var(--led)); white-space: nowrap; }
  @media (max-width: 860px) { .gk { margin-left: 0; } }
</style>
